.TH EBS-ORPHAN-BILL 1 "2026-11-19" "ebs-orphan-bill 1.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ebs-orphan-bill \- list loose EBS volumes and old snapshots without an AMI, with their monthly cost
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
Find loose EBS volumes and old snapshots without an AMI, and their monthly cost. Reads files only, no network.

Usage: ebs-orphan-bill [OPTIONS] <FILE>...

Arguments:
  <FILE>...  `describe-volumes`, `describe-snapshots`, or `describe-images` JSON. Files, gzip, directories, or `-` for standard input

Options:
      --prices <FILE>           Price file. The default is the built-in US East (N. Virginia) EBS list
      --as-of <YYYY-MM-DD>      Date ages are measured to, `YYYY-MM-DD`, midnight UTC. Without it, snapshots are not classified
      --older-than-days <DAYS>  A snapshot is old when its age is strictly greater than this many days [default: 30]
      --fail-usd <AMOUNT>       Exit 1 when a priced orphan's exact monthly USD is strictly greater than this
      --json                    Print JSON instead of the text table
      --md                      Print a Markdown report (tables) instead of the text table
      --output <FILE>           Write the report to this file instead of standard output. The file is replaced whole or not at all; a symlink or a non-regular file is refused
      --quiet                   Hide the warnings; print only the result and errors
      --verbose                 Print progress on standard error: each input read, then one line of counts
  -h, --help                    Print help
  -V, --version                 Print version

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 a priced orphan costs more than --fail-usd, 2 input or usage error, or standard output that cannot be written.
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
ebs-orphan-bill reads local JSON saved from
.BR "aws ec2 describe-volumes" ,
.BR "aws ec2 describe-snapshots" ,
and
.BR "aws ec2 describe-images" .
It lists the loose volumes and the old standard snapshots that no image references, with a monthly USD figure for each priced row.
It reads files only, makes no network call, and uses no AWS SDK.
The report identifies orphans and monthly cost.
It does not delete volumes or snapshots.
The monthly figure is an estimate from list prices, not the invoice: the text report has one estimate line and the totals are \fBestimated_usd_month\fR, and the JSON has \fB"estimate": true\fR.
.PP
A volume is loose when
.B Attachments
is missing or empty.
A snapshot is old when its
.B StorageTier
is standard or missing, its age is strictly greater than
.BR --older-than-days ,
and no image references it in
.BR BlockDeviceMappings[].Ebs.SnapshotId .
Without a
.B describe-images
document, snapshots are not classified as orphans.
Age is measured to
.BR --as-of ;
without it, volumes are still priced and snapshots are not classified.
.PP
Prices are the built-in US East (N. Virginia) rates for a full month of the provisioned size.
Magnetic standard volumes and archive snapshots are listed and not priced.
The free tier is not applied.
Snapshot cost is an upper bound.
.PP
An input is a file, a single gzip member (recognised by its first bytes, not by the file name), a directory, or
.B -
for standard input.
A directory is read recursively, at most 64 levels deep.
An input file may be at most 268435456 bytes, also after decompression.
A document may hold at most 16777216 JSON values plus object keys, or the command exits 2.
A file is read whole into memory, and memory use is a multiple of the file size that depends on the shape: about 10 times for a realistic export, about 54 times for 6000000 numbers in one array (a 12000023-byte file took about 619 MiB), and about 150 times for arrays nested 120 deep. The \-\-json report is written straight into one string; for 2320000 volumes it took 9.67 s and 2.4 GiB.
Because of the value limit, the memory of one document is bounded at about 5 GiB (measured for arrays nested 120 deep, the worst shape tried).
The size limit applies to each file; all files of one run are held in memory together.
Control characters, white space other than the plain space, and invisible and direction-changing characters in a file name and in an argument echoed in a usage error are shown as \fB\\u{..}\fR escapes.
In the price source, a control character is refused with exit 2, and the other hidden characters are shown as escapes.
A bad file in a directory stops the run with exit 2.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --prices \fIFILE\fR
Read the prices from this file instead of the built-in list.
The file has the same shape as the built-in quote.
.TP
.B --as-of \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR
The date ages are measured to, at midnight UTC.
The year runs from 1970 to 2199.
.TP
.B --older-than-days \fIDAYS\fR
A snapshot is old when its age is strictly greater than this many days.
The default is 30.
.TP
.B --fail-usd \fIAMOUNT\fR
Exit 1 when the exact monthly USD of a priced orphan is strictly greater than
.IR AMOUNT .
.I AMOUNT
is plain digits with an optional fraction, at most 40 characters, at most 28 decimals (zeros at the end count), and no more digits than a decimal holds exactly, or the command exits 2.
For a snapshot priced from its size in bytes the GB figure is rounded once, half away from zero, to the digits a decimal holds. The monthly amount is that figure times the rate, kept exact, and the comparison uses that exact amount.
.TP
.B --json
Print one JSON object on standard output instead of the text table.
.TP
.B --md
Print a Markdown report instead of the text table: the same amounts, one table for volumes and one for snapshots, the estimate line, and the totals. A pipe in a cell is escaped as \fB\e|\fR and a line break as a \fB\eu{..}\fR escape. It cannot be combined with \fB--json\fR.
.TP
.B --output \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to this file instead of standard output; standard output stays empty. The report is written to a temporary file in the same directory and renamed over \fIFILE\fR, so the file is the old one or the whole new one. An existing regular file is replaced. A symlink is refused and not followed, as is a directory or any file that is not a regular file. A missing directory is not created. A write error exits 2 with a message.
.TP
.B --quiet
Hide the warnings from the text report and from standard error. The result on standard output and the exit code do not change; the JSON object keeps its \fBwarnings\fR array.
.TP
.B --verbose
Print progress on standard error: one line per input read, then one line of counts. Standard output does not change. \fB--quiet\fR and \fB--verbose\fR together are a usage error (exit 2).
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print the usage text.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was written.
.TP
.B 1
.B --fail-usd
is set and a priced orphan costs more than that amount.
.TP
.B 2
An input or usage error, or standard output that is closed or cannot be written.
A closed standard output is recognised on Linux only.
The message goes to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ebs-orphan-bill --as-of 2026-11-09 examples/volumes.json examples/snapshots.json examples/images.json
ebs-orphan-bill --as-of 2026-11-09 --fail-usd 16 examples/volumes.json examples/snapshots.json examples/images.json
ebs-orphan-bill --as-of 2026-11-09 --json examples/volumes.json examples/snapshots.json examples/images.json
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
README.md and QUALITY.md in the source tree.
